From f685c6fdb432260761a0b31b4c3baaea945e290a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: reaper <theunbounddeveloper@outlook.com>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 17:10:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] warden: user-supplied blocklists, geo-before-feed
 attribution, and four backlog fixes

WARDEN CUSTOM FEEDS (new). rwarden_cfeeds holds up to 8 '<'-separated
name>url>url6 records. Each URL is fetched on the existing schedule and merged
into $NEW/$NEW6, so custom entries land in rw_threat/rw_threat6 and inherit the
bogon/LAN filter, the atomic swap, the jffs cache, the existing rules and the
existing counters - no new set, no new chain, no change to rule ordering.

The URL is the only user data that reaches the generated updater, where it goes
into a single-quoted curl argument alongside the curated table's fixed literals.
The charset is therefore a security boundary, not a tidiness check: ' would close
that quote, and \ ` " $ whitespace < > @ are excluded too. ; # ( ) ! * are also
excluded - inert inside single quotes, but the quoting is the only thing making
them safe and a future edit that emits the URL unquoted would turn ';' into a
command separator. Every value is re-validated at generation time as well as on
save, because nvram is not a trust boundary.

Verified by driving the real emission loop with hostile nvram: shell escape via
single quote, command substitution, backtick, a quote in the name and an http
downgrade all emit nothing; a hostile record between two valid ones is dropped
while its neighbours still emit; 12 records yield exactly 8. Validators pass 39
hostile cases including all five curated feed URLs, and rw_field is
canary-checked for overflow with oversized fields refused rather than truncated.

Also hardened, found by the same pass:
- the page bootstrapped rwarden_cfeeds raw into a single-quoted JS string; a
  stored apostrophe would have ended it. Now nvram_char_to_ascii +
  decodeURIComponent, as Reaper_Firewall.asp already does for its rule lists.
- curl -fsSL follows redirects and permits https->http, so an https feed that
  302'd to http was still followed and its content loaded into a DROP set.
  Now --proto =https --proto-redir =https, probed once so a curl without the
  flag still fetches (failing closed means no threat data at all).
- no --max-filesize, and /tmp is tmpfs: one oversized list was a
  memory-exhaustion event. Capped at 32 MiB.
- RW_CFEED_MAX counted successes, so a value full of junk was walked in full
  (2001 records = 2001 iterations, measured). It now bounds records parsed.

GEO BEFORE FEEDS (owner decision). The per-country rules are now evaluated
before the threat feed at all three emission sites - both source stacks and the
shared dst group used by RW_OUT and RW_SELF. Both still jump to the same drop
target, so this changes only which counter increments, never whether a packet is
dropped. The feeds ran first and overlap heavily with the countries people
block, so the feed absorbed nearly every hit while the country table sat near
zero - the "block count with no matching country count" field report. The trade,
taken knowingly: banked country counts now climb much faster and the feed count
flattens, leaving a discontinuity in accumulated history at this version. Every
comment that described the old order was corrected in the same commit.

BACKLOG FIXES:
- Reaper_Firewall.asp: the IPv6 protocol select submitted its visible text as
  its value, so tokenizing the labels - the obvious i18n fix - would have sent a
  translated word into ipv6_fw_rulelist, which rc/firewall.c compares with
  strcmp against "TCP"/"BOTH"/"OTHER". Values are now pinned with explicit
  value= attributes, defusing the trap before someone tidies the page.
- Reaper_Firewall.asp: RFW_36/RFW_37 were interpolated into a single-quoted JS
  string (standing rule 29); converted to backticks.
- Main_ReaperDash.asp: the Wi-Fi Encryption row linked to the per-radio Wireless
  page, which on a MULTILAN_MWL build cannot change what the row reports. It now
  targets SDN.asp, gated on isSupport('mtlancfg') exactly as menuTree.js is, and
  keeps the old destination as the fallback so a model without the SDN overlay
  cannot dead-end.
- Reaper_About.asp: the donate buttons pointed straight at two payment
  endpoints, which an image cannot ever change. One link to our own page now;
  the redirect happens in the browser, so the router still fetches, parses and
  validates nothing.

i18n: RWDN_77-85 minted and English-seeded into all 25 packs (6638 -> 6647,
lockstep). reaper_langcheck reports 0 breakers across 20 pages.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
